[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step checklist** for the two plugins you mentioned, plus the surrounding changes you’ll need to make (namespace, token, style URL, etc.).  
Everything is written as “**Mapbox → MapLibre**” so you can copy-paste the snippets into your existing code base.

---

## 1️⃣ Prerequisites – the “global” migration steps

| What you already have | What you must change first |
|----------------------|----------------------------|
| `import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl'` (or a `<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/.../mapbox-gl.js">`) | **Swap the core library** – see the “Package & namespace” section below. |
| `mapboxgl.accessToken = 'pk.…'` | **Remove the token** – MapLibre does not need it (and will error if you keep it). |
| `style: 'mapbox://styles/...` | **Replace the style URL** with a non-Mapbox source (e.g. OpenFreeMap, a self-hosted style, or a MapTiler style). See the “Style URL” section for a quick test URL. |
| CSS that references `mapboxgl-…` classes | **Rename to `maplibregl-…`** (or use the plugin-specific CSS fixes described later). |

> **If you haven’t done those global steps yet, do them now** – the plugin fixes assume the core library already works with MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Core library migration (quick recap)

```bash
# 1️⃣ Remove the old package
npm uninstall mapbox-gl

# 2️⃣ Install MapLibre
npm install maplibre-gl          # latest v6 (ESM-only)
# If you need a drop-in v1-compatible build for a short-term fix:
# npm install maplibre-gl@1
```

### Import / CSS

```js
// BEFORE (Mapbox)
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import 'mapbox-gl/dist/mapbox-gl.css';

// AFTER (MapLibre v6 – ESM)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
```

> **If you are using a bundler that still expects a default export**, you can do:
> ```js
> import {Map as MapLibreMap} from 'maplibre-gl';
> const map = new MapLibreMap(...);
> ```

### Create the map (example)

```js
// BEFORE
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,
  accessToken: 'pk.…'   // <-- remove
});

// AFTER
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` → drawing shapes

### 3.1 What works out-of-the-box

`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` **does not depend on any Mapbox-only API**, so the JavaScript part works unchanged **once the core namespace is swapped**:

```js
// BEFORE
import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';
const draw = new MapboxDraw({ /* options */ });
map.addControl(draw);
```

```js
// AFTER
import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';   // keep the same package
// OR, if you prefer a fork that already ships the CSS fixes, use:
// import MapLibreDraw from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-draw';
const draw = new MapboxDraw({ /* same options */ });
map.addControl(draw);
```

> **Key point:** the class name (`MapboxDraw`) stays the same; you only need to make sure the CSS class prefixes match what MapLibre renders.

### 3.2 CSS class fix (the only thing that usually breaks)

Mapbox GL renders controls with the prefix `mapboxgl-`. `mapbox-gl-draw` ships its own stylesheet that also uses that prefix (`.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw`). After the migration the map’s internal classes become `maplibregl-…`, so the draw UI appears **un-styled**.

#### Two ways to fix it

| Option | How to apply | When to use |
|--------|--------------|-------------|
| **A. Simple CSS override** (no extra package) | Add a small CSS block that maps the old class names to the new ones. | Quick fix, works for most projects. |
| **B. Use the MapLibre-maintained fork** | Install `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-draw` (a drop-in fork that already uses the `maplibregl-` prefix). | If you want a zero-maintenance solution and don’t mind an extra dependency. |

#### A. Simple CSS override (recommended if you already have a custom draw stylesheet)

```css
/* mapbox-gl-draw.css is still imported, but we rename the prefixes */
.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw {
  /* the control container – same as MapLibre expects */
}
.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw .mapboxgl-ctrl-icon {
  /* icons, etc. */
}

/* The only thing we need is to make the selector match the new prefix */
.maplibregl-ctrl-draw,
.maplibregl-ctrl-draw .maplibregl-ctrl-icon {
  /* copy the rules from the original .mapboxgl-… selectors */
}

/* If you import the original draw CSS, you can just add: */
.maplibregl-ctrl-draw { /* same rules as .mapboxgl-ctrl-draw */ }
.maplibregl-ctrl-draw .maplibregl-ctrl-icon { /* same rules as .mapboxgl-ctrl-icon */ }
```

A **minimal version** that works for the default UI:

```css
/* mapbox-gl-draw.css is already loaded */
.maplibregl-ctrl-draw {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  margin: 10px;
}
.maplibregl-ctrl-draw .maplibregl-ctrl-icon {
  background-size: 100%;
}
```

> **Tip:** If you’re using a CSS pre-processor (Sass, Less) you can simply do a global replace: `mapboxgl-` → `maplibregl-` in the draw stylesheet.

#### B. Use the MapLibre fork (no CSS changes)

```bash
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-draw
```

```js
import MapLibreDraw from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-draw';
const draw = new MapLibreDraw({ /* same options */ });
map.addControl(draw);
```

The fork ships a stylesheet that already uses the `maplibregl-` prefix, so you can keep the original CSS import:

```js
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-draw/dist/maplibre-gl-draw.css';
```

### 3.3 Verify it works

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // you should see the draw toolbar styled correctly
  console.log('Draw control added', draw);
});
```

If the toolbar appears but the icons are missing, double-check that the CSS file is being loaded (network tab) and that the class names match the ones in the stylesheet.

---

## 4️⃣ `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` → search box

### 4.1 Why you need a replacement

`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` is tightly coupled to **Mapbox’s Geocoding API** (it automatically appends the access token, expects the Mapbox response shape, and the default UI uses the `mapboxgl-` CSS prefix). After you remove the token and switch to a non-Mapbox style, the control will:

* still try to call `https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/...` → 401/403
* render with the wrong CSS prefix → unstyled

### 4.2 Two migration paths

| Path | What you install | How you configure | When to choose it |
|------|------------------|-------------------|-------------------|
| **A. Drop-in MapLibre fork** | `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` | Same API as the Mapbox version, but you **must supply your own geocoding endpoint** (Nominatim, Photon, MapTiler, etc.) | You want the exact same UI and code shape, just a different backend. |
| **B. Use a different UI library** (e.g., **MapLibre GL Geocoder** from the community, or a custom `<input>` + fetch) | Any third-party geocoder UI (e.g., `leaflet-geosearch`, `autocomplete.js`) | You can keep the UI you like, but you’ll need to write a tiny wrapper to feed results to the map. | You already have a custom UI or want a lighter-weight control. |

Below we focus on **Path A**, because it’s the most straightforward for a Mapbox-to-MapLibre migration.

### 4.3 Install the MapLibre fork

```bash
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

> The package name is `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`, **not** `@mapbox/...`. It ships the same class (`MapLibreGeocoder`) and the same CSS file (`maplibre-gl-geocoder.css`).

### 4.4 Import & CSS

```js
// BEFORE (Mapbox)
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

// AFTER (MapLibre)
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';
```

### 4.5 Provide a non-Mapbox geocoding endpoint

The fork does **not** embed a default endpoint; you must pass a `geocoder` option that implements the **Mapbox Geocoder API contract** (or use the built-in `MapLibreGeocoder` helper that talks to Nominatim/Photon). The easiest way is to use the built-in `MapLibreGeocoder` wrapper that ships with the package:

```js
// Example using the public Nominatim demo server (good for testing only)
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // Required: a function that returns a Promise of GeoJSON features
  // The wrapper already knows how to talk to Nominatim if you set `forwardGeocode`
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const response = await fetch(
      `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`
    );
    const data = await response.json();
    // Nominatim returns GeoJSON FeatureCollection already – just return it
    return {
      features: data.features
    };
  },
  // Optional: reverse geocode (for the “search this area” button)
  reverseGeocode: async (coords) => {
    const response = await fetch(
      `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/reverse?format=geojson&lat=${coords.lat}&lon=${coords.lng}`
    );
    const data = await response.json();
    return {
      features: [data]   // Nominatim returns a single Feature
    };
  },
  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  maplibregl: maplibregl,   //

**What’s happening**

1. **`map.loadImage` no longer accepts a callback** – starting with **MapLibre GL v4.0.0** the method returns a **Promise** instead of invoking a callback.  
2. Because the callback is never called, the image never gets added to the style, so any layer that references the image (or a custom `Marker` that you create with `map.addImage`) ends up with a missing-icon (you see a blank map or a missing pin).  

The missing icons are therefore a *code-migration* issue, not a problem with the sprite or the style itself.

---

## 1️⃣ Fix the `loadImage` call

### Old Mapbox-style code (callback)

```js
map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  map.addImage('pin', img);
});
```

### New MapLibre-style code (Promise)

```js
// Make sure the map’s style is fully loaded first
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    // `loadImage` resolves to an object { data: ImageBitmap }
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(url);
    // `addImage` expects the ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement)
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
  }
});
```

**If you prefer `.then()` syntax**

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage(url)
    .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
    .catch(err => console.error(err));
});
```

### Why the `await`/`.then` is required

- **MapLibre v4+** removed the callback signature from `loadImage`.  
- The method now **always returns a Promise** that resolves with `{ data: ImageBitmap }`.  
- Passing a callback is ignored, so the function you wrote is never executed → no image → missing marker.

---

## 2️⃣ Ensure the image is added **after** the style is ready

`map.addImage` can only be called **once the style has been parsed** (i.e., after the `load` event).  
If you call `loadImage` before the `load` event, the promise will still resolve, but `addImage` will throw:

```
Error: The style has not been loaded yet.
```

So wrap the whole thing in `map.on('load', …)` (or `map.once('styledata', …)` if you reload styles later).

---

## 3️⃣ Verify the URL you’re loading

- **Absolute URL** (e.g., `https://example.com/pin.png`) works out-of-the-box.  
- **Relative URL** works only if the file is served from the same origin as your page.  
- **`mapbox://` URLs** will *never* work in MapLibre – replace them with an HTTP(S) URL or a data-URI.

If you were using a Mapbox-hosted sprite (`mapbox://sprites/...`) for the icon, switch to a self-hosted sprite or a plain image file.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist for “marker icons are gone”

| ✅ Check | What to do |
|--------|------------|
| **`map.loadImage` uses a Promise** | Update the code as shown above. |
| **Call happens after `map.on('load')`** | Wrap the call in a `load` listener. |
| **URL is not `mapbox://`** | Use an `https://` URL or a data-URI. |
| **`addImage` id matches the layer’s `icon-image`** | If you have a layer like `{ "icon-image": "pin" }`, make sure you added the image with the same id (`'pin'`). |
| **Sprite / glyph URLs in the style are not `mapbox://`** | Replace any `sprite` or `glyphs` URLs in your style JSON with non-Mapbox URLs (e.g., OpenFreeMap style, your own sprite). |
| **CSS class names** | If you styled a custom HTML marker with `.mapboxgl-marker`, rename to `.maplibregl-marker` (or just use the default `new maplibregl.Marker()` which creates its own element). |

---

## 5️⃣ Full minimal example (ES-module build)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom pin</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { width: 100%; height: 100vh; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const pinUrl = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/custom-marker.png';

  map.on('load', async () => {
    try {
      const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(pinUrl);
      map.addImage('pin', img);

      // Add a layer that uses the image
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-pins',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [{
              type: 'Feature',
              geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] },
              properties: {}
            }]
          }
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'pin',
          'icon-size': 0.5,
          'icon-allow-overlap': true
        }
      });
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('❌ loadImage failed', e);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run this snippet (or copy it into a local file) and you should see a custom pin appear. If it works, the problem in your app is exactly the callback-to-promise mismatch.

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR – What you need to change in your code

```js
// BEFORE (Mapbox)
map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  map.addImage('pin', img);
});

// AFTER (MapLibre v4+)
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(url); // ← promise
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  }
});
```

Once you make that change (and verify the URL isn’t a `mapbox://` URL), the marker icons will appear again.  

If you still see blanks after the fix, double-check the **style JSON** for any lingering `mapbox://` sprite or glyph URLs, and make sure the `icon-image` property in the layer matches the id you passed to `addImage`.  

Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a quick-reference guide that shows **all the practical ways you can add a searchable geocoder box to a Mapbox GL JS v2 map** – from the built-in Mapbox solution to fully open-source alternatives you can self-host.  
I’ve grouped the options by the **service you call** (Mapbox vs. open) and then by the **UI component** you can drop into the map (official plugin, community fork, or “roll-your-own”). For each choice I note:

| What you get | How you add it | Token / API key needed? | Typical usage limits / cost | How it works with Mapbox GL JS v2 |
|--------------|----------------|------------------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------|

---

## 1️⃣ Use the **official Mapbox Geocoder** (the simplest, “out-of-the-box” option)

| What you get | How you add it | Token / API key needed? | Limits / cost | Remarks |
|--------------|----------------|------------------------|----------------|---------|
| A ready-made search box that autocompletes place names, addresses, POIs, and returns **Mapbox Geocoding API** results (including `bbox`, `center`, `place_name`, etc.). | Install the npm package (or load via CDN) and attach it to the map. | **Yes** – a Mapbox access token with the `geocoding:search` scope (the same token you already use for the map). | 100 k free requests / month on the free tier; paid plans are per-request. | Works with Mapbox GL JS v2 without any code changes. |
| **Installation (npm)** | ```bash
npm install @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
``` | | | |
| **Installation (CDN)** | ```html
<link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
``` | | | |
| **Basic usage** | ```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (you already have this)
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ Create the geocoder control
const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken, // same token as the map
  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,                // required for the built-in UI
  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  zoom: 14,                         // zoom to result
  flyTo: true,
  marker: false                     // we’ll add our own marker if we want
});

// 3️⃣ Add it to the map UI (top-left by default)
map.addControl(geocoder);

// 4️⃣ Optional: listen for the result event
geocoder.on('result', (e) => {
  console.log('Selected place:', e.result);
  // e.result.geometry.coordinates is [lng, lat]
});
``` | | | |
| **Customization** | • `countries`, `bbox`, `proximity`, `language`, `types` (address, poi, region, etc.)<br>• `flyTo: false` if you want to handle the camera yourself.<br>• `marker: true|false|{color:'…'}` to auto-add a marker.<br>• CSS can be overridden (`.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder`) to match your UI. | | | |
| **When to use** | • You already have a Mapbox token and are happy with Mapbox’s data quality.<br>• You need the **autocomplete** UI that shows suggestions as the user types.<br>• You want the simplest integration (few lines of code). | | | |

---

## 2️⃣ Use a **community-maintained fork** of the Mapbox plugin that works with **any** geocoding service

If you want to keep the same UI but point it at a non-Mapbox service (e.g., Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler Geocoding), the easiest path is the **MapLibre-compatible geocoder**:

| What you get | How you add it | Token / API key needed? | Limits / cost | Remarks |
|--------------|----------------|------------------------|----------------|---------|
| Same UI component (`MapboxGeocoder`-style) but **no hard-coded Mapbox URLs**. You supply a `fetch` function or a custom `geocoder` object that talks to any HTTP geocoding API. | Install `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (works with Mapbox GL JS v2 as well as MapLibre). | **Only if the service you choose requires a key** (e.g., MapTiler Geocoding). | Depends on the chosen service. | Great if you want to stay on Mapbox GL JS v2 but avoid Mapbox’s paid API, or if you’re already planning to migrate to MapLibre. |
| **Installation** | ```bash
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
``` | | | |
| **CDN** | ```html
<link href="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder@latest/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder@latest/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
``` | | | |
| **Basic usage with Nominatim** | ```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (same as before)
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN'; // still needed for the map itself
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ Create a tiny wrapper that talks to Nominatim
function nominatimGeocoder(query) {
  const url = new URL('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search');
  url.searchParams.set('q', query);
  url.searchParams.set('format', 'json');
  url.searchParams.set('addressdetails', 1);
  url.searchParams.set('limit', 5);
  // Nominatim requires a valid User-Agent / Referer – the CDN build adds one automatically.
  return fetch(url)
    .then(res => res.json())
    .then(data => data.map(item => ({
      // MapLibre-Geocoder expects the same shape as Mapbox Geocoding results
      place_name: item.display_name,
      center: [parseFloat(item.lon), parseFloat(item.lat)],
      geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [parseFloat(item.lon), parseFloat(item.lat)] },
      // optional: add a bbox if you want the map to fit the result
      bbox: item.boundingbox ? [
        parseFloat(item.boundingbox[2]), // west
        parseFloat(item.boundingbox[0]), // south
        parseFloat(item.boundingbox[3]), // east
        parseFloat(item.boundingbox[1])  // north
      ] : undefined,
      // any other properties you like
      properties: { osm_id: item.osm_id, osm_type: item.osm_type }
    })));
}

// 3️⃣ Instantiate the geocoder control, passing the custom function
const geocoder = new MaplibreGeocoder({
  // `geocoder` can be a function that returns a Promise of an array of results
  geocoder: nominatimGeocoder,
  placeholder: 'Search OSM…',
  maplibregl: mapboxgl, // required for the UI internals
  marker: false
});

// 4️⃣ Add it to the map
map.addControl(geocoder);

// 5️⃣ Listen for the result (same event name)
geocoder.on('result', (e) => {
  console.log('Nominatim result:', e.result);
});
``` | | | |
| **Other services** | • **Photon** (`https://photon.komoot.io/api/`) – similar wrapper, returns `features` array already in GeoJSON format, so you can just `return fetch(...).then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>d.features);`<br>• **Pelias** (`https://pelias.example.com/v1/search`) – same pattern.<br>• **MapTiler Geocoding** – requires an API key; you can use the built-in `MapTilerGeocoder` class that ships with `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`. | Depends on the service. | | |
| **Why use this fork?** | • You keep the **nice dropdown UI** without rewriting it.<br>• You can swap services at runtime (e.g., fallback to Nominatim if Mapbox quota is exceeded).<br>• Works with **Mapbox GL JS v2** (the plugin only needs the `mapboxgl`/`maplibregl` object for UI helpers). | | | |

---

## 3️⃣ Build a **custom UI** and call any geocoding API yourself

If you want full control over the look-and-feel, or you already have a UI framework (React, Vue, Svelte, plain HTML), you can:

1. **Create an input element** (or use a UI library component).  
2. **Debounce** the keystrokes (e.g., 300 ms).  
3. **Fetch** the geocoder endpoint of your choice.  
4. **Parse** the response into a list of suggestions.  
5. **Render** the suggestions (dropdown, list, etc.).  
6. **When a suggestion is clicked**, call `map.flyTo({center: [lng, lat], zoom: …})` or `map.jumpTo(...)` and optionally add a marker.

### Minimal vanilla-JS example (using Nominatim)

```html
<div id="search-wrapper" style="position:absolute;top:10px;left:10px;z-index:1;">
  <input id="search-input" type="text" placeholder="Search…" style="width
